{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792893927,"nonce":"RvoW7a24QxRCMiUiEVRGCgjRXy8=","title":"A memory dump's marks make the next collection free live objects","message":"hl.Gc.dumpMemory() marks the heap and leaves the object marks standing, on the\nreasoning that a mark only retains and the next sweep clears it. But the next\ncollection marks before it sweeps, and its trace does not follow an object it\nfinds already marked. Anything such an object came to reach after the dump\n(an array's grown storage, a field written since) goes unmarked and is freed\nwhile reachable.\n\nAsh survives the same reasoning because a major there clears every mark first\n(clear_all_marks), and its minors treat marked objects as old with the card\nbarrier catching later writes. Caribou's heap has neither: its marks are\ncleared only by the sweep.\n\nReproducer (Haxe): a static array of one element; hl.Gc.dumpMemory(); push\n20000 three-element arrays (the static's storage grows after the dump);\nhl.Gc.major(); churn 200000 arrays; read the 20000 back. Caribou 1ca2bcc:\n\"Uncaught exception: Null access\" at the read; ash: all 20000 intact. The\nChimpMonk editor probe with MEMORY_DUMP crashed the same way (\"SetArray: index\n0 out of bounds (size=0)\" with the reload room, exit 139 without) a stage after\nits dumps.\n\nFix: the dump withdraws its object marks before the world restarts. A hosted\ncollector's claims are made and ended inside one hold of the GC lock, which\nthe dump also takes, so none can be standing when it runs. Line marks stay;\nthey only retain until the next sweep.","files":null}]}